WEBVTT

00:00:00.265 --> 00:00:01.367
Neurodiversity.

00:00:01.953 --> 00:00:04.959
Neurodiversity describes the
idea that people experience

00:00:04.984 --> 00:00:07.894
and interact with the world
around them in different ways.

00:00:08.433 --> 00:00:12.460
There is no one right way of thinking,
learning, and behaving, and these

00:00:12.461 --> 00:00:14.888
differences are not to
be viewed as deficits.

00:00:15.451 --> 00:00:17.120
It's important for
all of us to foster an

00:00:17.145 --> 00:00:19.709
environment that is
conducive to neurodiversity.

00:00:20.397 --> 00:00:24.480
We need to recognize and emphasize each
and every person's individual strengths

00:00:24.481 --> 00:00:28.484
and talents, and provide support for their
differences and requirements.

00:00:29.117 --> 00:00:31.255
People with neurological
differences are not

00:00:31.256 --> 00:00:33.860
broken or incomplete
versions of normal people.

00:00:34.027 --> 00:00:36.400
We're all born different
and raised differently.

00:00:36.674 --> 00:00:40.380
Hence, all of us are to
some extent differently abled.

00:00:40.979 --> 00:00:44.935
Neurodiversity is a normal and natural
variation in the human genome.

00:00:45.701 --> 00:00:47.723
The distinctiveness of
neurodiverse people

00:00:47.802 --> 00:00:50.601
isn't something that
necessarily needs to be cured.

00:00:51.091 --> 00:00:54.563
Neurodiverse employees merely
need some help and accommodation.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:56.791
They may bring new
perspectives to an

00:00:56.792 --> 00:00:59.541
organization's efforts to
create or recognize value.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:02.990
In this course, you will
recognize the value that

00:01:02.991 --> 00:01:05.661
neurodiverse employees
add to an organization.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.934
You'll understand how to
educate your employees about

00:01:08.959 --> 00:01:12.113
neurodiversity and create
an inclusive workplace.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.743
You'll learn to examine
strategies for providing

00:01:14.932 --> 00:01:17.321
equal access to
neurodiverse employees.

00:01:17.801 --> 00:01:19.889
Neurodiversity in the Workforce

00:01:20.655 --> 00:01:24.560
The neurodiverse population remains
a largely untapped pool of talent.

00:01:25.139 --> 00:01:27.760
Unemployment runs as high as 80%.

00:01:28.862 --> 00:01:31.033
Even when they're
working, highly capable

00:01:31.058 --> 00:01:34.280
neurodiverse people
are often underemployed.

00:01:34.695 --> 00:01:37.077
Many times, despite
having required skill sets

00:01:37.132 --> 00:01:40.534
and solid credentials, they
have to settle for menial jobs.

00:01:41.128 --> 00:01:43.203
These poor numbers
are due to the way

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:45.609
companies screen and hire
their possible candidates.

00:01:46.417 --> 00:01:51.560
Fostering an inclusive workplace begins
with making neurodiversity a priority.

00:01:52.827 --> 00:01:57.340
Employers and recruiters need to revamp
their hiring practices, so as not to miss

00:01:57.341 --> 00:02:00.767
out on any of the neurodiverse talent that
could help the company flourish.

00:02:01.369 --> 00:02:04.140
Recruitment of people on the
spectrum is only the first step.

00:02:04.748 --> 00:02:08.501
Employers need to make certain
accommodations for a fulfilling work

00:02:08.526 --> 00:02:10.560
environment for people
across the spectrum.

00:02:11.234 --> 00:02:15.920
Encouraging neurodiversity makes
organizations inclusive and innovative.

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:19.810
Employees who identify on
the neurodiverse spectrum

00:02:19.811 --> 00:02:22.321
bring with them new
ways of looking at things.

00:02:22.872 --> 00:02:25.805
This encourages the entire
team to think creatively.

00:02:26.282 --> 00:02:30.680
Merging unique perspectives and ideas
within a team naturally fosters an

00:02:30.681 --> 00:02:33.200
environment that is innovative and
forward-thinking.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.908
Advantages of neurodiversity
at the workplace

00:02:37.784 --> 00:02:41.741
Managers claim that inclusive
practices are already showing benefits

00:02:41.766 --> 00:02:44.260
in ways far beyond
reputational enhancement.

00:02:45.404 --> 00:02:49.320
These include productivity gains,
quality improvement, boost in innovative

00:02:49.321 --> 00:02:51.680
capabilities, and growth in employee
engagement.

00:02:52.887 --> 00:02:57.143
They've begun thinking more deeply about
leveraging the talents of all employees

00:02:57.296 --> 00:02:59.980
through greater sensitivity
to individual needs.

00:03:01.202 --> 00:03:06.268
Better pattern recognition Large
companies, particularly tech companies,

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:09.900
have realized that they already had
neurodiverse talent on their staff.

00:03:10.816 --> 00:03:14.407
Many of these employees contribute their
special skills in pattern recognition,

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:20.971
memory, or mathematics on teams working
to solve highly complex problems.

00:03:21.643 --> 00:03:25.395
In the fields of machine learning,
artificial intelligence, and data science,

00:03:25.645 --> 00:03:30.621
neurodiverse employees have proven to
outperform their neurotypical counterparts.

00:03:31.272 --> 00:03:32.617
Creative thinking

00:03:33.023 --> 00:03:36.013
Variations in their neurological
constitution allows

00:03:36.068 --> 00:03:40.358
neurodivergent employees to think
differently or out of the box.

00:03:41.491 --> 00:03:45.006
They're able to see past the
conventional frameworks which

00:03:45.031 --> 00:03:47.901
could limit the creativity
of neurotypical individuals.

00:03:48.498 --> 00:03:51.532
Positive work environment or
organization's reputation

00:03:52.575 --> 00:03:55.834
According to the training industry,
employers who have opened their doors

00:03:55.893 --> 00:04:00.110
to a neurodiverse workforce have
found that neurodiverse and autistic

00:04:00.135 --> 00:04:03.825
employees have a retention
rate upwards of 90%.

00:04:04.528 --> 00:04:08.100
Moreover, having neurodiverse
talent in your workforce

00:04:08.101 --> 00:04:11.884
improves a company's external
as well as internal image.

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:16.640
When a brand has a strong purpose,
consumers are four times more likely to

00:04:16.641 --> 00:04:21.300
trust the company and six times more likely
to defend the company from criticism.

00:04:21.996 --> 00:04:24.709
Accommodations for
neurodivergent employees

00:04:25.502 --> 00:04:28.729
Organizations as well as employees
can benefit from creating

00:04:28.753 --> 00:04:31.701
a thoughtfully designed
accessible workspace.

00:04:32.411 --> 00:04:36.403
It opens up a creative sandbox where
people are free to think, work,

00:04:36.497 --> 00:04:39.175
and create in a way that 
works best for them.

00:04:40.109 --> 00:04:41.629
Inclusive recruiting

00:04:42.149 --> 00:04:45.680
For people on the neurodiversity
spectrum, interviews can be

00:04:45.681 --> 00:04:48.469
a challenge because their
communication styles are

00:04:48.494 --> 00:04:51.400
not similar to those of
neurotypical candidates.

00:04:52.158 --> 00:04:56.840
The robust communication and eye contact
that recruiters and employers look for is

00:04:56.841 --> 00:04:58.933
not the strong suit of
candidates on the spectrum.

00:04:59.238 --> 00:05:01.540
Try to diversify your hiring strategy.

00:05:02.512 --> 00:05:05.462
Cyclometric assessments
are far more objective as they

00:05:05.463 --> 00:05:08.533
eliminate human biases in
early stages of recruitment.

00:05:09.432 --> 00:05:12.820
Provide multiple opportunities to
candidates so that they can demonstrate

00:05:12.821 --> 00:05:17.933
their ability and competency during the
process, regardless of neurodiversity.

00:05:19.261 --> 00:05:21.526
Formalize a hiring
program to help create a

00:05:21.527 --> 00:05:24.461
talent pipeline of
neurodivergent candidates.

00:05:25.246 --> 00:05:26.860
Distraction-free environment

00:05:27.485 --> 00:05:30.100
Provide a distraction-free
workspace to employees.

00:05:30.125 --> 00:05:34.457
For example, noise-canceling headphones to
reduce sensory overload and spacious

00:05:34.482 --> 00:05:37.626
drawers and storage cabinets
to declutter their desks.

00:05:38.095 --> 00:05:41.130
Distraction need not
necessarily be physical.

00:05:41.833 --> 00:05:44.000
Mental decluttering 
is also as important.

00:05:45.041 --> 00:05:49.620
Provide reference materials, worksheets,
and interview questions in advance and

00:05:49.621 --> 00:05:51.981
have sufficient mental health 
days in your company calendar.

00:05:52.271 --> 00:05:56.350
Assistive technologies can help some
neurodivergent employees to perform better.

00:05:57.162 --> 00:06:01.640
Such support can include software and
devices like portable and talking word

00:06:01.641 --> 00:06:05.592
processors, assistive listening 
systems, and visual organizers.

00:06:06.605 --> 00:06:08.605
Architectural accommodations

00:06:09.116 --> 00:06:11.460
You can create focused 
areas of work where

00:06:11.461 --> 00:06:16.400
employees, neurodiverse or neurotypical,
can choose to work with low stimuli.

00:06:17.283 --> 00:06:21.803
Assess the texture of desks, chairs,
and any other high-touch surfaces.

00:06:22.584 --> 00:06:26.682
Spatial disorientation can frustrate
people with dyslexia and autism.

00:06:27.557 --> 00:06:32.660
So try to create unique spaces with visual
landmarks and clear lines of sight that

00:06:32.661 --> 00:06:35.669
can help everyone map their
own location within a space,

00:06:36.132 --> 00:06:38.539
ultimately saving
both time and frustration.

00:06:39.882 --> 00:06:41.809
Arranging furniture
in different ways can

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:44.060
also help people feel
better equipped at work.

00:06:44.676 --> 00:06:48.036
The same positioning of the desk
might not be comfortable for everyone.

00:06:48.786 --> 00:06:51.100
So allow a space where
 pacing is welcomed.

00:06:51.914 --> 00:06:53.452
Flexibility

00:06:53.476 --> 00:06:56.396
Consider giving your employees
the option of remote working.

00:06:57.177 --> 00:07:00.640
Flexible work schedules support your
entire workforce and not just

00:07:00.641 --> 00:07:05.280
neurodivergent employees, working parents,
caregivers, and people with disability.

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:08.477
Empowering language

00:07:08.664 --> 00:07:11.040
Neurodiversity advocates
encourage inclusive,

00:07:11.065 --> 00:07:12.400
non-judgmental language.

00:07:13.315 --> 00:07:17.588
While many disability advocacy
organizations prefer person-first language,

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:22.020
a person with autism, a person with
Down syndrome, some research

00:07:22.021 --> 00:07:25.019
has found that the majority
of the autistic community

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:28.616
prefers identity-first
language, an autistic person.

00:07:29.429 --> 00:07:34.159
Therefore, rather than making assumptions,
it's best to ask directly what a person's

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.753
preferred language is and how they want to
be addressed.

00:07:39.057 --> 00:07:40.240
Educate your team.

00:07:41.064 --> 00:07:43.525
Create a culture where
individuals feel comfortable to

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:46.590
disclose and talk openly
about their neurodiversity.

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:51.146
Upscale your workforce by providing
diversity and inclusion training days

00:07:51.412 --> 00:07:53.958
for all so that everyone
understands the challenges

00:07:53.982 --> 00:07:56.601
and the uniqueness
of neurodiversity.

00:07:57.260 --> 00:08:01.019
Focus on fostering an open and trusting
relationship with your team.

00:08:01.730 --> 00:08:03.920
Ask the employees what support they need.

00:08:04.782 --> 00:08:07.617
Provide employees additional
support and guidance if required.

00:08:08.028 --> 00:08:11.374
And ensure to make employees aware
of the availability of the same.

00:08:12.515 --> 00:08:14.177
Ensure other team
members are aware of the

00:08:14.202 --> 00:08:16.940
specific needs each
individual may require.

00:08:17.323 --> 00:08:21.040
Moreover, handle performance
issues very sensitively.

00:08:21.267 --> 00:08:24.995
If you feel uncertain about anything,
you can begin by asking your employees

00:08:25.035 --> 00:08:28.787
what they feel is required for them to
work efficiently and comfortably.

00:08:29.725 --> 00:08:32.047
Your employees do not
have to reveal their medical

00:08:32.048 --> 00:08:34.548
history for you to make
accommodations at the workplace.

00:08:35.188 --> 00:08:37.602
You can simply ask them to share
which elements of their work

00:08:37.627 --> 00:08:40.988
experience could be made
more inclusive or accessible.

00:08:41.722 --> 00:08:45.820
By the end of this course, you've learned
neurodiversity describes the idea that

00:08:45.821 --> 00:08:49.473
people experience and interact with the
world around them in many different ways.

00:08:49.879 --> 00:08:52.742
There is no one right way
of thinking, learning, and

00:08:52.743 --> 00:08:56.465
behaving, and differences
should not be viewed as deficits.

00:08:57.324 --> 00:09:01.260
You learned that the neurodiverse
population remains a largely untapped pool

00:09:01.261 --> 00:09:04.841
of talent, and they have the ability to
bring in new perspectives.

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:09.300
You learned that neurodivergent employees
are proven to have better pattern

00:09:09.301 --> 00:09:13.658
recognition and creative thinking than
their neurotypical counterparts.

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:16.748
Plus, accommodating
neurodivergent candidates

00:09:16.772 --> 00:09:18.985
can greatly improve
a company's image.

00:09:19.719 --> 00:09:23.760
You learned that people managers must
practice inclusive recruiting with,

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:27.667
for example, psychometric assessments
or performance-based tests

00:09:27.692 --> 00:09:30.285
so as not to miss out
on neurodivergent talent.

00:09:30.793 --> 00:09:35.420
You learned that leaders must accommodate
neurodivergent employees by creating

00:09:35.421 --> 00:09:40.039
distraction-free workspaces, providing
flexibility, and educating themselves

00:09:40.064 --> 00:09:44.203
and their team about the challenges and
uniqueness of people on the spectrum.